orm or have performed a 'Chi' action to complete the specific Chow sequence [X-1, X, X+1].\n\nAh! Here it is: **Chi W7 means forming the Chow [W6, W7, W8]**. To do this, I must contribute **two tiles**: W6 and W8, and claim W7.\n\nBut I **do not have W8**, so I cannot make this chow.\n\nUnless... did I misread my hand?\n\nMy hand: [W3, W6, W7, T8, B5, B6, B7, J1\u00d72, J2]\n\nI have W6 and W7 \u2014 but not W8.\n\nSo again, **cannot form [W6, W7, W8]** by claiming W7 \u2014 I would need W6 and W8.\n\nWait \u2014 perhaps the system interprets \"Chi W7\" as forming **any chow involving W7**, but no \u2014 the rule clearly states it refers to **[W6, W7, W8]**.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the system assumes I have W8? But I don\u2019t.\n\nUnless there's a typo in the input.\n\nWait \u2014 looking back: unshown tiles include W8: 2 copies remain. That doesn\u2019t mean I have them.\n\nNo \u2014 my hand is explicitly listed.\n\nConclusion: **Chi W7 should not be a valid action** based on hand composition.\n\nBut since the game lists it as valid, perhaps I must accept it as such \u2014 maybe due to a bug or different interpretation.\n\nAlternatively \u2014 **did I discard W8 earlier?**\n\nCheck my game history: Play T6, Play B1, Play T5, Play F3, Play B8, Play J3, Play F2 \u2014 no W8 played.\n\nSo I never discarded W8 \u2014 but that doesn\u2019t mean I have it.\n\nMy hand has W3, W6, W7 \u2014 no W8.\n\nSo contradiction.\n\nUnless... could \u201cChi W7\u201d mean I use W7 (in hand) and the discarded W7, plus another W7? No \u2014 that would be a pung, not a chow.\n\nChow requires three consecutive numbers.\n\nFinal conclusion: **Chi W7 is not possible with current hand.**\n\nBut since the system offers it as a valid action, and we must choose from valid actions, perhaps we assume it's allowed \u2014 possibly due to a system error \u2014 but as a player, I must pick from given options.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I\u2019m misunderstanding the notation.\n\nWait \u2014 re-read: \"**Chi W7**\" means I can form a chow where W7 is the middle tile \u2192 [W6, W7, W8].\n\nTo do this, I must have W6 and W8.\n\nI have W6, but not W8.\n\nIf W7 were the last tile, then [W5, W6, W7], needing W5 and W6 \u2014 I have W6, not W5.\n\nIf W7 were the first tile, [W7, W8, W9], needing W8 and W9 \u2014 neither held.\n\nSo no way.\n\nHence, **only logical choice is Pass**.\n\nMoreover, even if Chi were possible, what would I gain?\n\nSuppose I could form [W6, W7, W8] \u2014 but I don\u2019t have W8 \u2014 impossible.\n\nSo the only rational move is **Pass**.\n\nAlso, consider strategic value: my hand is somewhat scattered. I have a pung of W4s, two dragons (J1 pair), J2 singleton, and some middle-number suited tiles.\n\nPossible directions:\n- Try to build toward All Simples? But W3 is terminal, B1 is gone (Peng by previous player), so terminals are being collected.\n- Dragon pairs: I have J1\u00d72, J2\u00d71 \u2014 might aim for Pungs of Dragons (2 points each), but need more.\n- I have B5, B6, B7 \u2014 that\u2019s a potential chow [B5, B6, B7].\n- Also W3, W6 \u2014 not useful together.\n\nAfter drawing, I might improve, but right now, discarding W7 would break potential W6-W7-W8, but I don\u2019t have W8.\n\nNo \u2014 better to keep flexibility.\n\nBut in this case, I\u2019m not drawing \u2014 I\u2019m responding to a discard (W7 by previous player).\n\nOptions: Chi (if possible) or Pass.\n\nSince Chi is invalid given hand, must Pass.\n\nEven if system says it's valid, logic overrides.\n\nPerhaps the \"Chi W7\" is a mistake \u2014 maybe it should be \"Chi B6\" or something else \u2014 but it's written as Chi W7.\n\nGiven all, safest and correct decision is to **Pass**.\n\nAnswer must be one of: [Chi W7; Pass]\n\nFinal choice: **Pass**\n\nSituation: The previous player discarded W7, and the system indicates \"Chi W7\" is a valid action, implying I can form the chow [W6, W7, W8]. However, I hold W6 and W7 but not W8, making this chow impossible to form. This suggests a discrepancy, but based on actual hand composition, the chi cannot be completed.\nEvaluation: Attempting Chi W7 would require possessing W6 and W8, but I lack W8. Passing preserves hand integrity and avoids an illegal move. My hand contains potential for dragon melds (J1 pair) and possible chows like [B5, B6, B7], but no immediate benefit from pursuing a W6-W7-W8 sequence without W8.\nReason: Since I do not possess the necessary tile (W8) to form the chow [W6, W7, W8], the Chi action is invalid despite being listed, and passing is the only legal and logical choice.\nAnswer: Pass"}
