\u2014 but W4 is already used in pung \u2192 impossible.\n\nTherefore, the only viable path is to form [W5,W6,W7] as a chow and use W3\u00d72 as pair.\n\nSo I should **discard W3** (one of them).\n\nNow check scoring:\n\nPotential patterns:\n- All Chows? No \u2014 I have two pungs ([W4\u00d73], [J1\u00d73]) \u2192 not all chows.\n- All Pungs? No \u2014 I have a chow [B4,B5,B6] and will have another chow [W5,W6,W7] \u2192 only two pungs.\n- Mixed Triple Chow? Need three identical-number chows in different suits \u2014 no.\n- Pure Shifted Chows? Three successive chows in same suit \u2014 I have only one chow so far in W.\n- Half Flush? Yes! All melds are in W (characters) and B (dots), and J1 (dragon). Wait \u2014 suits: W, B, and honor \u2192 multiple suits.\n\nWait: tiles involved:\n- W: W4\u00d73, W5, W6, W7, W3\u00d72 \u2192 yes\n- B: B4,B5,B6 \u2192 yes\n- Honor: J1\u00d73\n\nSo three types of suits present \u2192 not Full Flush nor Half Flush.\n\nBut consider: \u201cMixed Shifted Pungs\u201d? No \u2014 not pungs.\n\nWait \u2014 what about **Triple Pung**? Three pungs of same number in different suits? No \u2014 W4, J1, different numbers.\n\nNo strong pattern apparent.\n\nBut let\u2019s calculate minimal score:\n\n- Dragon Pung (J1): 2 points\n- One Voided Suit? I have W (characters), B (dots), and T (bamboos) \u2014 wait! Do I?\n\nMy melds:\n- [W4\u00d73] \u2192 character\n- [B4,B5,B6] \u2192 dots\n- [J1\u00d73] \u2192 dragon (honor)\n\nAnd hand: W3, W5, W6, W7 \u2192 all character\n\nBut opponent melds include T2-T3-T4, T6-T7-T8, T9\u00d73 \u2192 so bamboo exists on table, but not in my hand.\n\nIn my hand: only W and B and J1.\n\nSuits in my hand: Characters (W) and Dots (B), and Honors (J1)\n\nSo two suited suits (W and B) + honor \u2192 not one-suited \u2192 so **One Voided Suit**: yes, since I lack Bamboo (T) in my hand \u2192 counts for 1 point.\n\nAlso:\n- Outside Hand? Requires every meld and pair to contain terminal or honor. But:\n  - [W4\u00d73]: W4 is simple \u2192 not terminal/honor \u2192 fails\n- So no.\n\nOther possibilities:\n- Melded Hand: all melds are exposed (yes \u2014 all via Peng), and if I win by claiming a discard, then yes \u2192 6 points\n- Self Drawn: if I draw winning tile myself \u2192 1 point, but incompatible with Melded Hand\n\nSo best chance: aim for Melded Hand (6 points) + Dragon Pung (2 points) = 8 points \u2192 minimum to win.\n\nIs there any other fan?\n\n- Pung of Terminals or Honors: J1 is dragon \u2192 already counted under Dragon Pung\n- J1 pung also qualifies as general honor pung, but Dragon Pung supersedes\n\nSo core: Dragon Pung (2) + Melded Hand (6) = 8 \u2192 sufficient.\n\nTo achieve Melded Hand:\n- Must win by claiming a discard (not self-draw)\n- And all melds must be exposed \u2014 they are (Peng x2, Chi x1? Wait!)\n\nWait \u2014 critical error: **[B4,B5,B6]** was formed by **Chi B5** \u2014 yes, in my history: \"Chi B5\"\n\nBut **Chi** means I claimed B5 from a discard to form a chow \u2014 so it is **exposed**, yes.\n\nAnd **Peng W4**, **Peng J1** \u2014 both exposed.\n\nSo all three melds are exposed \u2192 good.\n\nNow, to complete my hand, I need to form fourth meld: [W5,W6,W7] as chow \u2014 but that requires me to either:\n- Draw W5/W6/W7 to complete it, then win by self-draw \u2192 but then **Melded Hand** fails (because Melded Hand requires *all* melds and the pair to be completed by other players' tiles \u2014 including the winning tile)\n\nWait \u2014 definition:  \n> *Melded Hand*: Every meld and pair in the hand must be completed by tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be exposed, and **one tile of the pair is the 14th winning tile from other players.**\n\nAh! So even the pair must be completed by a discard \u2014 i.e., I must have single W3, then claim the second W3 from a discard to win.\n\nBut currently I have **W3\u00d72** in hand \u2014 so the pair is already formed internally.\n\nTherefore, if I keep W3\u00d72, then the pair is self-formed \u2192 **Melded Hand cannot be achieved**.\n\nHence, to qualify for Melded Hand, I must discard one W3, keep only one W3, and later claim the second W3 from a discard to complete the pair.\n\nThen:\n- All melds are exposed (\u2713)\n- Winning tile (second W3) comes from opponent\u2019s discard (\u2713)\n\u2192 Melded Hand (6 points)\n\nAdditionally:\n- Dragon Pung (J1) \u2192 2 points\nTotal: 8 points \u2192 valid win.\n\nBut if I instead keep W3\u00d72 and win by self-draw, then:\n- Dragon Pung (2)\n- Self Drawn (1)\n- Possibly other small fans?\n\nBut 3 points only \u2014 not enough.\n\nNeed at least 5 more.\n\nCheck:\n- One Voided Suit (I have W and B and J1 \u2014 missing T? Wait \u2014 my hand has only W and B? No: my melds: W4, B4-B5-B6, J1 \u2192 suits: W, B, honor \u2192 yes, missing bamboo (T) in *my* hand \u2192 so One Voided Suit: 1 point\n- All Simples? No \u2014 J1 is honor \u2192 breaks it\n- No Honors? No \u2014 has J1\n- Mixed Double Chow? Do I have two chows in different suits with same numbers? I would have [B4,B5,B6] and [W5,W6,W7] \u2014 overlapping numbers but not identical \u2192 no\n- Short Straight? Two chows in same suit with successive numbers? If I have [W5,W6,W7] and say [W3,W4,W5] \u2014 but W4 is used in pung \u2192 impossible\n\nSo no additional significant fans.\n\nThus:\n- Option A: Discard W3, keep one W3, wait to claim second W3 \u2192 enables Melded Hand (6) + Dragon Pung (2) = 8\n- Option B: Keep W3\u00d72, aim to complete [W5,W6,W7] by self-draw \u2192 then only ~3\u20134 points \u2192 insufficient\n\nTherefore, **must discard one W3** to enable Melded Hand.\n\nNow, among valid actions: [Play W3; Play W5; Play W6; Play W7]\n\n\"Play X\" means discard X.\n\nSo I should **Play W3**\n\nBut which W3? I have two \u2014 discarding one leaves one.\n\nYes.\n\nAlso, risk assessment:\n- Is W3 safe to discard? Other players have discarded many W1, W2, W8, W9 \u2014 but few W3.\n- Unshown W3: 4 out of 4 remain unseen \u2014 meaning none revealed yet? Wait: total W3: 4 copies. None in any meld visible? Let's check:\n\nMy hand: W3\u00d72 \u2192 so two in hand  \nUnshown: W3: 4 \u2192 implies total unplayed W3 is 4, but I have 2 \u2192 so 2 still in wall/unseen \u2192 so 2 W3 already discarded or in other hands?\n\nWait: total tiles per type: 4 copies.\n\nCount known W3:\n- My hand: 2\n- Discarded or melded elsewhere? Check game histories:\n\nOthers\u2019 discards:\n- Next player: Play W9, W6, W7, W1, W1 \u2192 no W3\n- Across: Play W9, W2, W2, W8 \u2192 no W3\n- Previous: Play W4, W6, W7, W2, W8, W9, W5, W3 \u2192 **Play W3** once\n\nAh! Previous player discarded W3 once.\n\nSo one W3 discarded.\n\nTotal:\n- In play: previous player discarded one W3\n- In my hand: two W3\n\u2192 so three accounted for\n\u2192 one W3 left in wall\n\nSo danger: someone may be waiting for W3 to complete a chow like [W1,W2,W3], [W2,W3,W4], or [W3,W4,W5]\n\nBut given many W1, W2, W4, W5 have been played, likelihood is low.\n\nMoreover, if I don\u2019t discard W3, I block my own chance to get Melded Hand.\n\nSo better to take controlled risk.\n\nReason: To enable the \"Melded Hand\" pattern (6 points), which requires the winning pair to be completed by claiming a discard. Currently holding two W3s completes the pair internally, disqualifying this pattern. By discarding one W3, I leave myself with a single W3, allowing future completion of the pair via an opponent's discard. Combined with the existing Dragon Pung (2 points), this achieves exactly 8 points, the minimum required to win.\n\nAnswer: Play W3"}
