n 2: **Play W6**\n  - Keeps B7.\n  - B7 appears in the next player\u2019s meld [B6,B7,B8], meaning one B7 is already used there.\n  - Total B7 count: 4 copies. One in opponent\u2019s chow, one in my hand \u2192 2 accounted for. Unshown says B7:2 \u2192 so 2 remain unseen.\n  - But again, I need a **pair**, not a single.\n  - However, B7 may be dangerous \u2014 the next player already used it in a chow, and they might be open on terminals or building toward flush. Also, holding B7 doesn\u2019t lead to any new chows unless I get B5/B6/B8 \u2014 but B8 is already all gone (0 left), so no new chow with B7 can be formed.\n  - Therefore, B7 also cannot form any further chow.\n\nNow consider scoring patterns:\n- I already have:\n  - Three pungs of W4, W5, J1 \u2192 possibly heading toward **All Pungs** (6 points).\n  - All three melds are concealed (Peng W4, Peng W5, Peng J1 \u2014 wait: Peng means exposed! So all are **exposed pungs**).\n    - So **Fully Concealed Hand** is broken.\n    - But **All Pungs** (6 pts) still possible if I form another pung.\n    - Also, I have three different pungs: two in Characters (W4, W5), one Dragon (J1).\n    - Could aim for **Triple Pung** (16 pts): three pungs/kongs of same number in different suits? No \u2014 these are different numbers.\n    - Or **Mixed Shifted Pungs**? W4, W5 \u2192 would need W6 \u2192 then pungs of W4/W5/W6? But that\u2019s same suit \u2192 not mixed.\n    - Alternatively, **Pure Straight**? Already have [B4,B5,B6] chow \u2192 if I had [W1,W2,W3] etc., no \u2014 not applicable.\n- More promising: **Upper Tiles** (24 pts): only 7,8,9 suited tiles. But I have W4, W5 \u2014 too low.\n- **Middle Tiles**? W4, W5 \u2014 yes, but also have B4\u2013B6 \u2192 possible, but W6 is middle tile (6), B7 is upper.\n- Actually, most of my hand is mid-to-upper, but mixed.\n\nBut here\u2019s a key point: I have **three exposed pungs**: W4, W5, J1. If I can get a fourth pung, even exposed, I can score **All Pungs (6 pts)**. Combined with other patterns?\n\nAlso note:\n- Prevailing Wind is East (F1), Seat Wind is North (F4).\n- I have no wind pungs \u2014 but I have J1 (Red Dragon) pung \u2192 qualifies for **Dragon Pung (2 pts)**.\n- No F1 or F4 pung \u2192 no Prevalent or Seat Wind pattern.\n- But if I form a pair of W6 or B7, neither is wind/honor, so no bonus there.\n\nHowever, recall: **Chicken Hand** is worth 8 pts \u2014 defined as a legal hand with otherwise zero fans, but this is a fallback.\n\nBut my hand already has:\n- All Pungs (if completed): 6 pts\n- Dragon Pung (J1): 2 pts\n\u2192 Total: 8 pts \u2192 qualifies to win.\n\nSo if I can complete a fourth pung and a pair, I can win legally.\n\nBut right now, I don\u2019t have a pair. So I must discard one tile and hope to draw the other half later.\n\nCritical observation:\n- I currently have **only two single tiles**: W6 and B7.\n- After discarding one, the other becomes a singleton \u2014 I will need to draw its duplicate to make a pair.\n- Remaining tiles: W6 has 2 left (out of 4), B7 has 2 left \u2014 equal chance.\n\nBut danger:\n- B7: Next player used it in a chow [B6,B7,B8] \u2192 they may be building a **Full Flush (Bamboo)** or **Upper Tiles**, and might be waiting for B7 to win.\n- Since they already have B6,B7,B8, they likely have more Bamboos.\n- If I keep B7, and later draw another B7, I can form a pair \u2014 but if I discard it now, they might win by claiming it (but only if it completes their hand).\n- But in this case, they just completed a chow with B7 \u2014 unlikely that B7 alone completes their hand unless they are waiting for pair.\n- Still, safer to **not feed opponents**.\n\nBut conversely, W6:\n- Is less dangerous \u2014 no one has shown interest in W6.\n- W5 is already tripled (I have a pung), so others may avoid W6.\n- And W6 can help me form a **concealed pair** later.\n\nMoreover, unshown W6: 2 left \u2192 good chance to draw.\n\nBut here\u2019s a strategic consideration:\n- I have W4, W5 pungs \u2014 very close to **Pure Shifted Pungs** (W4, W5, W6) \u2014 if I can form a pung of W6!\n- That pattern is worth **24 points**.\n- Currently I have W4 and W5 pungs \u2014 if I can promote W6 into a pung, I get Pure Shifted Pungs (24 pts).\n- Even better: that would give me:\n  - Pure Shifted Pungs (24)\n  - All Pungs (6)\n  - Dragon Pung (2)\n  \u2192 Total \u2265 32 pts \u2014 excellent score.\n\nTherefore, **keeping W6 supports a high-value pattern**.\n\nWhereas B7 leads nowhere \u2014 no significant pattern with a B7 pung.\n\nThus, **discarding B7 is better** \u2014 keep W6 for potential W6 pung and **Pure Shifted Pungs**.\n\nReason: Discarding B7 preserves the possibility of forming a pung of W6, which would complete the high-scoring **Pure Shifted Pungs** (24 pts) in Characters along with existing W4 and W5 pungs. This path offers significantly higher scoring potential than keeping B7, which has no such synergy. Additionally, B7 is riskier as it was recently used by the next player in a chow.\n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
