lly draw a pair. With only T1 and T7 in hand, and no duplicate, I must discard one and hope to draw a match.\n\nNow compare safety and potential:\n\n- **T1**: Still 3 copies left. Not visibly dangerous. Previous player has T9 pung, but not T1. Next player discarded T1 earlier \u2014 so they likely don\u2019t want it. Others haven't shown interest. Safer to keep.\n- **T7**: T7 appears in a chow by the player across (T6-T7-T8), who already completed that meld. They also had T7 in hand and discarded T7 \u2014 indicating they may be safe on T7. But T7 is higher value in sequences.\n\nBut here's the critical point: **There are no valid winning actions listed** like Win or Gang \u2014 only discards: Play T1 or Play T7. So I\u2019m not winning this turn. I must discard.\n\nSo decision: Which discard gives me better chance to complete a winning hand?\n\n- Keeping **T1** allows possibility of forming **Lower Tiles (1,2,3)** pattern if I draw more T1s or related tiles. But T2 is rare (only 1 left), T3 is gone (0 left). So no chance for chow involving T2-T3. Thus, only use for T1 is as a **pair** or **pung**.\n- Same for T7: T6 has 1 left, T8 has 2 left \u2014 possible for chow, but I already have four melds \u2014 I need a **pair**, not another chow.\n\nThus, goal is simply to form a **pair**.\n\nBoth T1 and T7 are equally viable for pairing. But consider **scoring patterns**:\n\n- Do I have any terminal/honor involvement?\n  - I have pungs in W4 (not terminal), W5 (not), J1 (dragon).\n  - No terminal pungs.\n- **All Pungs?** Almost \u2014 3 pungs, 1 chow \u2192 if I could replace the chow with a pung, yes. But can't now.\n- **Half Flush?** Only if all suited tiles in same suit \u2014 not true.\n- **Outside Hand?** Requires every meld to contain terminal or honor. But B4-B5-B6 is middle tiles \u2192 disqualifies.\n- **All Simples?** No \u2014 I have W4, W5, B4-B6 \u2014 all simples (2-8), but J1 is dragon (honor) \u2192 disqualifies.\n- So likely scoring comes from:\n  - Dragon Pung (2 pts)\n  - Possibly Melded Hand (6 pts) \u2014 since all my melds were formed via Peng (exposed), and if I win by stealing, but I need a pair.\n\nWait \u2014 check **Melded Hand**: All melds and the pair must include at least one tile from another player. My melds: Peng W4, Peng J1, Peng W5, Chi B5 \u2192 all exposed. The chow used a discarded tile (Chi B5). So all melds are exposed. If I complete the **pair** by taking a discard (i.e., someone discards a T1 or T7 and I claim to win), then **Melded Hand (6 points)** applies.\n\nAdditionally, if I win by stealing, I get **Chicken Hand (8 points)** as baseline.\n\nChicken Hand (8) + Melded Hand (6) = 14 \u2014 well over 8.\n\nAlso, Dragon Pung (2) \u2192 total 16+.\n\nSo even a simple win on a pair by stealing is valuable.\n\nTherefore, strategy: **Keep either T1 or T7, wait to complete pair, win by stealing (Peng or just pair-up).**\n\nNow decide which to discard.\n\nLook at **tile danger**:\n\n- T1: Previous player has Peng\u2019d T9, but never shown T1. Next player discarded T1 \u2014 so likely safe.\n- T7: Player across has Chi T7 (in T6-T7-T8), and also discarded T7 \u2014 so they may be safe on T7 now.\n\nBut T7 was discarded by multiple players \u2014 including next player and player across \u2014 suggesting it's relatively safe.\n\nBut **T1 has more survival potential** \u2014 could support lower-number patterns, though limited.\n\nMore importantly: **Unshown T1: 3 copies**, T7: 3 copies \u2014 equal availability.\n\nBut **B1 is all gone**, W1 all gone \u2014 so no one else building around 1s.\n\nSimilarly, T9 has 1 left \u2014 previous player has pung, so likely holding onto them.\n\nBut no indication anyone is waiting on T1 specifically.\n\nFinal consideration: **Future draw probability**.\n\nSince wall is thin, drawing either is uncertain. But discarding **T7** keeps **T1**, which is more consistent with early-game tendencies (previous player collected terminals).\n\nMoreover, **T1 is less likely 